// @test: run
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0,1
// @tags: newtype, field-access, archive, file, regression
// @timeout: 60000
// @expected-exit: 0
// @expected-stdout-begin
// nonneg=true plausible=true
// @expected-stdout-end
//
// T1192. A value RETURNED FROM A BAKED FUNCTION loses the reading of
// its own field. `File.create(path)` stores `fd.as_raw()` into
// `File.fd`, and what arrives there is not the descriptor:
//
//     f.as_raw_fd()   >= 0      true      <- every validity guard passes
//                     <  4096   FALSE     <- it is a heap address
//
// BOTH TIERS, and that is the point of this spec. The tier-1 half was
// visible (`fd.0` reads 4391403520 where tier 0 reads {3}); the tier-0
// half hides behind the interpreter's rendering, and only arithmetic
// exposes it — measured on a `FileDesc` from `safe_open`:
//
//     fd.0 == 3     false
//     fd.0 >  0     TRUE
//     fd.0 +  1     50851175201
//
// So `if fd >= 0 { … }` — the shape every caller writes — is satisfied
// by a pointer, and the wrong descriptor travels on in silence. That is
// why the failure surfaces far from its cause: `core.io.file.write`
// reports `write failed` while the file it created sits on disk, empty.
//
// FIVE CONTROLS, none of which reproduce, which is what narrows this to
// the ARCHIVE boundary rather than to newtypes, generics or Result:
//
//     FileDesc(3).0                          3    local construction
//     FileDesc(i32v as Int).0                3    an Int32 payload
//     fn f() -> FileDesc { FileDesc(3) }     3    a stdlib type returned
//                                                 from a USER function
//     fn f() -> Result<FileDesc, Text>       3    the Result wrapper
//     a user's own `type MyFd is (Int)`      3    newtypes as such
//
// Rebuilding does not help either — `FileDesc(fd.as_raw())` is equally
// wrong, because `as_raw()` already returns the damaged value. The
// corruption enters at the RETURN from a baked function and survives
// both an explicit annotation and a re-wrap.
//
// THIS SPEC IS RED AT BOTH TIERS TODAY. It is a guard written ahead of
// its fix — the same shape `array-len-survives-a-reference.vr` had for
// T1269 — so the day it goes green is measured rather than announced.
// `plausible` is the line that matters: `nonneg` is already true and
// proves nothing, which is exactly the property that let this defect
// travel.

mount core.prelude.*;
mount core.io.{File};

fn main() {
    let p: Text = "/tmp/verum-spec-fd-plausible.txt".to_text();
    match File.create(&p) {
        Ok(f) => {
            let r: Int = f.as_raw_fd();
            // A real descriptor is a small non-negative integer. The
            // upper bound is deliberately generous: any process limit
            // in use is far below it, and a heap address is far above.
            print(f"nonneg={r >= 0} plausible={r < 4096}");
        }
        Err(_) => print("create failed"),
    }
}
